<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Fun Outdoors!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like to Swim.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple responses to texts. Interactive: Write simple messages to peers using words, drawings, and short phrases.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can respond to short texts by writing simple sentences (e.g., "I like this story about running."). Can write simple messages to peers using words, drawings, and short phrases about outdoor activities (e.g., drawing a soccer ball and writing "Let's play!"). Can express simple reactions to texts using words or phrases (e.g., "Fun story!").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of outdoor activities: a child swimming, running, playing soccer, and climbing. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and encourages students to say "Swim!", "Run!", "Play soccer!". The teacher then points to a flashcard of swimming and says, "I like to swim. It's fun!" and invites students to repeat chorally. This transitions into writing by asking students to think about their own preference.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a "Happy Face" flashcard and says, "I feel happy when I swim quickly." The teacher writes the sentence "I swim quickly." on the board, pointing to each word and emphasizing 'quickly' as "how I swim." The teacher then writes "I like to run fast." The teacher circles 'swim' and 'run' and asks students to listen for the /s/ and /r/ sounds. (Phonemic awareness: /r/ in "run", /s/ in "swim").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do I like to do?" (Pointing to "I like to run fast.") Expected answer: "Run."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'fast' a doing word (verb) or a 'how' word (adverb)?" Expected answer: "'How' word / Adverb."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Fun Outdoors!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large "Class Outdoor Activity Book" with simple pictures of children doing different activities in a park or playground and short captions below each picture.
                    <br>Example Captions:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of a child swimming) "This is Mia. She likes to swim. She swims slowly."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of a child running) "This is Ben. He loves to run. He runs fast."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of children playing soccer) "They like to play soccer. It is a fun game."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students recognize and understand simple written descriptions about outdoor activities and preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads each caption aloud, pointing to the words.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the book about?" (Expected: "Outdoor activities / Sports / Fun.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> The teacher asks students to "Point to the picture where someone runs fast." or "Show me 'swim slowly'."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Circle the word:</b> Students receive a worksheet with the sentences. They circle the verb and underline the adverb in each sentence, e.g., "She <span style="text-decoration: underline;">swims</span> <span style="font-weight: bold;">slowly</span>." (Teacher models first).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Subject + verb + adverb to describe actions (e.g., "I run fast."), Present simple (e.g., "I love to run.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> run, jump, swim, play, race, like, love, watch, pass, fast, slow, happy, fun, quickly, slowly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Initial and final sounds with letter correspondence, specifically /r/ and /s/.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Expressing preferences for activities: "I like to run. It's fun."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of outdoor activities and incomplete sentences. They complete the sentences using words from a word bank (e.g., swim, run, play, fast, slow, fun).
                            <br>Example: (Picture of child swimming) "I like to ______. I swim ______." (Word bank: swim, slowly, run, fast, play, fun).
                            <br>(Picture of child running) "She likes to ______. She runs ______." (Teacher reminds students about /s/ and /r/ sounds as they write).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Sentence Building):</b> Students are given sentence starters and a choice of verbs and adverbs. They combine them to create their own sentences.
                            <br>Example:
                            <br>Start: "I like to..."
                            <br>Verbs: (swim, run, play soccer)
                            <br>Adverbs/Adjectives: (fast, slowly, happy, fun)
                            <br>Students write sentences like: "I like to swim. I swim slowly." or "I like to play soccer. It is fun."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check (Simple Message Writing):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student writes one simple message to their partner on a small card, describing an outdoor activity they like using a word or short phrase and a small drawing.
                            <br>Example 1: (Drawing of a soccer ball) "Let's play!"
                            <br>Example 2: (Drawing of a child running) "I run fast!"
                            <br>They exchange cards. Partners check if they understand the message and the drawing.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Fun Outdoors!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> "My Favorite Outdoor Activity Postcard."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Students receive a template for a postcard. On one side, they draw a picture of themselves doing their favorite outdoor activity (e.g., swimming, running in the park, playing with a ball). On the other side, they write 2-3 simple sentences about it, expressing their preference and describing the action using a verb and an adverb/adjective. They can "send" this postcard to a friend (another student) in the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Instructions:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw yourself doing your favorite outdoor activity.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Write: "I like to [verb]." (e.g., "I like to swim.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Write: "I [verb] [adverb/adjective]." (e.g., "I swim fast." or "It is fun.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Optional: "My friend, [friend's name], likes to [verb] too!"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Students use target grammar (Subject + verb + adverb, Present simple) and vocabulary (swim, run, play, fast, fun, park, friend) to produce a short, personalized written product, simulating a real-world message to a friend.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist based on student's "My Favorite Outdoor Activity Postcard."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Favorite Outdoor Activity Postcard."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes at least one simple sentence expressing preference (e.g., "I like to swim.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least one target verb (e.g., swim, run, play).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least one target adverb or adjective (e.g., fast, slowly, fun).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Drawing clearly represents the written activity.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Message is understandable (clear writing, basic spelling).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can write about what I like to do outside. (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My words are easy to read. (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite word to write today about outdoor activities? Why?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy or difficult about writing your postcard?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? Were the instructions clear for 2nd graders?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face with writing simple sentences and using adverbs? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next writing activity? (e.g., more sentence frames, specific word banks, peer support).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the lesson effectively address the specific objective and learning outcomes for writing?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The written postcards from this lesson will be used in Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration) as part of a class "Outdoor Activities Showcase" where students will share and explain their friends' preferences to another group or the class. This will involve mediating information from written text to oral communication.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>